                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the capability gaps`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `The 360 data, psychometrics and sponsor interviews are in. The <em>presenting</em> problems are slow decisions and silos after a difficult merger and a regulatory crisis. But those are symptoms — the <strong>underlying capability gaps</strong> are leaders low in self-awareness, business units that do not lead collectively, and a senior team that freezes or over-controls under ambiguity. A credible diagnosis, not a menu of activities, is the foundation of a defensible design. Name the priority gap, trace it to the evidence, and be explicit about what you will deliberately <strong>not</strong> try to fix — because trying to fix everything dilutes everything.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Blend the three development elements`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Design the learning architecture by weighting the three elements — <strong>self-awareness</strong>, <strong>collective leadership through teams</strong>, and <strong>navigating complexity</strong> — in proportion to your diagnosis. A self-awareness-heavy design for a silo problem, or a complexity-lab-heavy design for a self-awareness gap, is a <em>mis-blend</em>. The merger silos were the sponsor's number-one gap, so a design with almost no collective work leaves the real problem untouched. Then sequence the 9-month journey so the elements reinforce one another rather than firing at random.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Allocate delivery formats within £600k`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Translate the blend into formats and cost it against the <strong>£600,000</strong> ceiling. The formats that transfer best — <strong>action-learning, coaching, immersive labs</strong> — are the most expensive, so a fully high-transfer design for all 120 runs to ~£840k, about 40% over. A cheap, faculty-led design fits with room to spare and <em>changes nothing back at work</em>. One-to-one coaching for all 120 alone is ~£250k. Engineer a mix that serves the blend and maximises transfer-to-practice per pound — and decide where to scale (cohorts, peer-coaching, digital) so the deep formats land where they matter most.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Co-create and defend the design`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `The board sponsor is in the room — sceptical, outcome-focused, and allergic to “training that feels nice and changes nothing.” Present the design as a co-created solution: the diagnosis, the blend, the format allocation, the budget — and, critically, <strong>how capability uplift and transfer will be measured</strong> at the 9-month review. He will push you to “just run some inspiring keynotes for all 120.” A programme everyone loved and no one changed because of is a failure here. Build the measurement into the design, not after it — and hold the line.`,

                    r1_priority_label:      `Which capability gap does the programme target as its PRIMARY priority?`,
                    r1_symptom_label:       `How do you separate the presenting symptom from the underlying capability gap?`,
                    r1_descope_label:       `Be explicit: which gap will the programme deliberately NOT try to fix this time?`,
                    gap_collective:         `Collective leadership across the merged businesses — the silos exposed by the merger and regulatory crisis`,
                    gap_selfaware:          `Self-awareness — leaders technically excellent but low in insight into their own impact`,
                    gap_complexity:         `Navigating complexity — the senior team that freezes or over-controls under ambiguity`,
                    gap_everything:         `All of them at once — the programme should fix every gap the data surfaced`,
                    symp_evidence:          `Trace each presenting symptom (slow decisions, silos) to the underlying gap in the 360 and psychometric evidence before naming any activity`,
                    symp_menu:              `Skip the diagnosis — go straight to a menu of inspiring activities the client will recognise and like`,
                    symp_sponsor:           `Just ask the sponsor what he wants and design exactly that, symptom or not`,
                    desc_named:             `Name one lower-priority gap to descope — trying to fix everything dilutes everything`,
                    desc_none:              `Descope nothing — promise the sponsor we will close all three gaps fully`,

